Keyword	Think
  
FROM	Where data comes from
JOIN	Combine tables
WHERE	Remove rows
GROUP BY	Create buckets
HAVING	Remove buckets
SELECT	What to show

  SELECT
  c.country,
  COUNT(o.order_id) AS total_orders,
  SUM(o.amount) AS revenue
FROM customers c
INNER JOIN orders o
  ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
WHERE o.amount >= 50
GROUP BY c.country
HAVING SUM(o.amount) >= 500
ORDER BY revenue DESC;

Read this in English:

1️⃣ Join customers to orders
2️⃣ Remove small orders
3️⃣ Group by country
4️⃣ Keep only strong countries
5️⃣ Rank them

This is real analyst SQL.



  Think of SQL as a pipeline.
SQL always runs in this order (this matters a LOT):

FROM → JOIN → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY


If you understand this order, everything clicks.
